## Reflection

**Which architecture decision involved the most important trade-off? Which requirement drove the decision, and what trade-off did you accept?**

ADR2, the explicit evidence sufficiency check, involved the most important trade-off. It was driven by RAI3 (abstain when evidence is insufficient) and supports RAI1 (groundedness), which I identified as the most important evaluation criterion. A system that always fills in every field will sometimes invent a deadline or a requirement, and a student may not notice before relying on it. The check lets ScholarshipScan say "not stated" instead, and the student can then open the source document to confirm.

The trade-off I accepted is false abstentions. A stricter check reduces invented values but also marks fields as "not stated" when the document does contain them, which makes the table less complete and adds a little processing time. This is why RAI3 has two thresholds (≥ 90% correct "not stated" and ≤ 15% false "not stated"), and why the check's strictness will have to be tuned during evaluation rather than fixed now.
